// Elliptic curves over prime fields: the arithmetic under ECDH and ECDSA,
// for P-256, P-384, P-521, secp256k1, the three Brainpool curves and SM2.
//
// One implementation for every curve, rather than one per curve, because
// the parts that go wrong are the same parts every time -- the reduction,
// the special cases of addition, the scalar loop -- and are better written
// once and checked against eight curves than written eight times.
//
// ## WARNING -- what this is not
//
// The arithmetic below is written to be **constant-time in the source**: no
// branch and no memory access depends on a secret. Field elements are fixed
// arrays of limbs; reductions subtract with a mask, not an `if`; point
// addition uses complete formulas, so adding a point to itself or to
// infinity takes the same path as any other addition; the scalar loop is a
// Montgomery ladder of a fixed length with a masked swap.
//
// **Grasm cannot promise the compiler preserves that.** Cranelift is free
// to turn a mask into a branch, as `x25519` says of itself. So this is
// written correctly and is *not audited*: do not protect anything that
// matters with it until the language can promise constant time and someone
// has reviewed this file.
//
// ## Representation
//
// A field element is `L` limbs of 26 bits, least significant first, held in
// Montgomery form (`aR mod p` with `R = 2^(26L)`). Twenty-six bits is the
// most that lets a limb product (52 bits) plus a second product and the
// carries stay inside a signed 64-bit integer, so no multiply is ever split.
// P-256 is ten limbs, P-521 twenty-one.
//
// Points are projective `(X : Y : Z)`, and addition is Algorithm 1 of
// Renes, Costello and Batina, "Complete addition formulas for prime order
// elliptic curves" (2016), which is correct for every pair of inputs on a
// curve of prime order -- and every curve here has cofactor 1, which the
// generator asserts. The parameters are read out of OpenSSL by
// `scripts/gen_curves.py`, not typed. The field arithmetic is `modarith`'s.
import modarith

type EcCurve = {
    name: str,
    f: ModField,
    n: ModField,
    a: [i64],
    b: [i64],
    b3: [i64],
    gx: [i64],
    gy: [i64],
}

type EcPoint = {
    x: [i64],
    y: [i64],
    z: [i64],
}

type EcError =
    | UnknownCurve(name: str)
    | BadEncoding
    | NotOnCurve
    | AtInfinity
    | BadScalar

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Curves

fn from_hex(text: str) -> bytes {
    var t = text
    if t.len() % 2 == 1 {
        t = "0" + t
    }
    var out: [i64] = []
    var i = 0
    while i + 1 < t.len() {
        out.push(t.substr(i, 2).parse_int_base(16) ?? 0)
        i = i + 2
    }
    bytes_of(out)
}

